Present value translates future cash into today's equivalent; future value compounds today's cash forward.|PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)^n.|₹121 in two years at 10% has present value ₹100.|The rate must match risk and cash-flow timing.|At a higher discount rate, this present value…?|Falls|Rises|Stays ₹100
Compounding moves value forward; discounting reverses that operation.|Multiply forward and divide backward by the same growth factor.|₹100 × 1.1 = ₹110; ₹110 ÷ 1.1 = ₹100.|Do not subtract the rate from the future amount to discount.|₹110 discounted one year at 10% equals…?|₹100|₹99|₹121
A timeline places each receipt and payment at its actual date; discount factors price each dated unit.|Discount factor at t = 1 ÷ (1 + r)^t.|At 10%, a year-two ₹100 receipt contributes ₹82.64 today.|A payment today has discount factor one.|Which receipt has greater PV at a positive rate?|₹100 in year one|₹100 in year two|Both are equal
Ordinary annuities pay at period-end; annuities due pay at period-start.|PV due = PV ordinary × (1 + r).|Two ₹100 end-year payments at 10% have PV ₹173.55; beginning-year payments ₹190.91.|Count the number of payments separately from their timing.|Moving every payment one period earlier makes PV…?|Higher at a positive rate|Lower|Unchanged
A growing annuity has a finite sequence of cash flows growing at a constant rate.|PV = C1/(r − g) × [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + r))^n], when r differs from g.|Payments ₹100 and ₹105 discounted at 10% have PV ₹177.69.|The first payment is C1; handle r = g using the limit or individual discounting.|If growth rises with risk unchanged, expected PV generally…?|Rises|Falls|Must become infinite
A perpetuity continues indefinitely; a growing perpetuity requires a sustainable growth rate below its discount rate.|PV = C1 ÷ (r − g).|Next-year cash ₹10, required return 10%, growth 2% gives ₹125.|Using current cash instead of next-period cash understates the growing formula.|If growth rises to 3%, value becomes approximately…?|₹142.86|₹100|₹333.33
Uneven cash flows must each be discounted at their own date.|PV = sum of Ct ÷ (1 + r)^t.|₹50 in year one and ₹121 in year two at 10% total ₹145.45 today.|Averaging flows can destroy their timing information.|Which method is appropriate?|Discount each payment separately|Treat both as today|Use only the final receipt
Nominal quoted rates differ from effective annual rates when compounding occurs within a year.|EAR = (1 + nominal/m)^m − 1; continuous EAR = e^r − 1.|12% nominal compounded monthly gives about 12.68% effective annually.|A nominal annual quote is not a monthly rate.|Monthly rate for this quote is…?|1%|12%|12.68%
Nominal rates include inflation; real rates measure purchasing-power growth.|1 + real rate = (1 + nominal rate) ÷ (1 + inflation).|8% nominal return and 5% inflation imply about 2.86% real return.|Subtracting inflation is only an approximation.|For unchanged nominal return, higher inflation makes real return…?|Lower|Higher|Unchanged
Amortising payments contain interest on the outstanding balance and principal repayment.|Payment = P × r ÷ [1 − (1 + r)^−n].|₹100 for two annual payments at 10% requires ₹57.62; first-year interest is ₹10.|Use the period rate, not an annual rate for monthly payments.|As principal declines in a level-payment loan, interest normally…?|Declines|Rises automatically|Equals the full payment
A sinking fund accumulates regular contributions toward a dated obligation.|FV ordinary annuity = payment × [(1 + r)^n − 1] ÷ r.|Two end-year savings of ₹100 at 10% accumulate ₹210.|Returns and contribution timing determine the required deposit.|Saving at each year's beginning instead gives…?|₹231|₹200|₹190.91
NPV is the present value of incremental benefits less incremental costs.|NPV = sum of discounted net cash flows, including time-zero spending.|Pay ₹100 now and receive ₹115 next year at 10%: NPV ₹4.55.|A project's discount rate must reflect its risk.|At a 15% rate, this project's NPV is…?|Zero|₹15|₹4.55
IRR makes NPV zero; MIRR specifies financing and reinvestment assumptions for negative and positive flows.|Compare NPV at the required return when rankings conflict.|A ₹100 outlay followed by ₹120 next year has IRR 20%.|Non-conventional cash flows can produce multiple IRRs or none.|Which rule better ranks different-size mutually exclusive projects?|NPV at an appropriate rate|Highest IRR always|Shortest name
Money-weighted return reflects investor cash-flow timing; time-weighted return links subperiod performance independently of external flows.|TWR = product of subperiod growth factors − 1.|A manager gains 10% then loses 10%: TWR is −1%, regardless of deposits between periods.|IRR and TWR answer different performance questions.|Which is better for isolating manager returns from client deposit timing?|Time-weighted return|Money-weighted return always|Total ending balance alone
Discount rates must match cash-flow frequency, currency, inflation basis, and ownership claim.|Nominal cash needs nominal rates; equity cash needs equity rates.|Monthly rupee payments need a consistent monthly rupee discount rate.|Currency conversion alone does not justify reusing an unrelated rate.|Should real cash flows use an inflation-inclusive nominal rate unchanged?|No|Yes|Only when positive
